  <header class="topbar wrap"><a class="brand" href="#top" style="text-decoration:none">Island of Song</a><a href="#composers">Explore the composers ↓</a></header>
  <section class="hero" id="top"><div class="wrap hero-inner"><div class="eyebrow">A listening guide · 1850—1950</div><h1>Meet the voices<br>behind the music.</h1><p>From Elgar’s soaring melodies to Britten’s bold operas, discover a century of British composers and the stories woven into their music.</p><a class="button" href="#composers">Explore the collection</a><p class="hero-note">Six composers. A hundred years of changing sound. Start anywhere.</p></div></section>
  <main class="wrap" id="composers">
    <div class="intro"><div><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#94632f">The collection</div><h2>Six lives in music</h2></div><p>These composers carried British music from the late Victorian age through two world wars. Each found a different way to turn tradition, landscape and everyday life into sound.</p></div>
    <div class="filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter composers"><button class="active" data-filter="all">All composers</button><button data-filter="romantic">Romantic &amp; lyrical</button><button data-filter="folk">Folk &amp; landscape</button><button data-filter="modern">Modern voices</button></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <article class="card" data-tags="romantic"><div class="card-top"><span class="initial">E</span><span class="years">1857–1934</span></div><h3>Edward Elgar</h3><div class="tag">The late Romantic</div><p>Born near Worcester, Elgar was largely self-taught. His richly expressive orchestral music brought him international recognition; the <em>Enigma Variations</em> and Cello Concerto remain among his best-loved works.</p><div class="listen"><span>Try: <em>Nimrod</em></span><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Elgar+Enigma+Variations+Nimrod+orchestra" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Listen ↗</a></div></article>
      <article class="card" data-tags="romantic"><div class="card-top"><span class="initial">S</span><span class="years">1858–1944</span></div><h3>Dame Ethel Smyth</h3><div class="tag">Composer &amp; campaigner</div><p>An adventurous composer of operas, chamber music and choral works, Smyth was also a leading suffragette. Her <em>The March of the Women</em> became an anthem of the movement.</p><div class="listen"><span>Try: <em>The March of the Women</em></span><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethel+Smyth+March+of+the+Women" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Listen ↗</a></div></article>
      <article class="card" data-tags="folk"><div class="card-top"><span class="initial">D</span><span class="years">1862–1934</span></div><h3>Frederick Delius</h3><div class="tag">Impressionist colours</div><p>Delius developed a distinctive, atmospheric style shaped by a life spent across several countries. Works such as <em>On Hearing the First Cuckoo in Spring</em> evoke nature through shifting orchestral colour.</p><div class="listen"><span>Try: <em>First Cuckoo</em></span><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Delius+On+Hearing+the+First+Cuckoo+in+Spring" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Listen ↗</a></div></article>
      <article class="card" data-tags="folk"><div class="card-top"><span class="initial">V</span><span class="years">1872–1958</span></div><h3>Ralph Vaughan Williams</h3><div class="tag">Folk song &amp; open skies</div><p>Vaughan Williams collected English folk songs and drew on older musical traditions to create a voice of his own. The <em>Fantasia on a Theme by Thomas Tallis</em> and <em>The Lark Ascending</em> are lyrical landmarks.</p><div class="listen"><span>Try: <em>The Lark Ascending</em></span><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vaughan+Williams+The+Lark+Ascending" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Listen ↗</a></div></article>
      <article class="card" data-tags="modern folk"><div class="card-top"><span class="initial">H</span><span class="years">1874–1934</span></div><h3>Gustav Holst</h3><div class="tag">Teacher &amp; innovator</div><p>Holst taught for much of his life and explored influences from English folk song to Sanskrit literature. His orchestral suite <em>The Planets</em> made a lasting impression, but his catalogue reaches far beyond it.</p><div class="listen"><span>Try: <em>Jupiter</em></span><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Holst+The+Planets+Jupiter+orchestra" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Listen ↗</a></div></article>
      <article class="card" data-tags="modern"><div class="card-top"><span class="initial">B</span><span class="years">1913–1976</span></div><h3>Benjamin Britten</h3><div class="tag">Opera &amp; new beginnings</div><p>One of the most influential British composers of the twentieth century, Britten wrote operas, orchestral and chamber works, and music for young performers. <em>Peter Grimes</em> established his international reputation.</p><div class="listen"><span>Try: <em>Peter Grimes</em> — Four Sea Interludes</span><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Britten+Four+Sea+Interludes+Peter+Grimes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Listen ↗</a></div></article>
    </div>
    <section class="about"><div><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#94632f">A note on the era</div><h2>One island, many musical worlds.</h2></div><p>“British music” is not a single sound. These composers ranged from Elgar’s grand Romantic sweep to Smyth’s spirited activism, Delius’s impressionistic hues, Vaughan Williams’s folk-inspired melodies, Holst’s bold experiments and Britten’s clear-eyed modernism. This small collection is a starting point, not a definitive list.</p></section>
